1
00:00:04,771 --> 00:00:08,428
On the afternoon of 27 March 1977,

2
00:00:08,919 --> 00:00:14,180
two Boeing 747s collided on a runway
on the Spanish island of Tenerife.

3
00:00:15,611 --> 00:00:17,465
Five hundred and
eighty-three people died.

4
00:00:18,036 --> 00:00:21,213
It is still the deadliest
accident in aviation history,

5
00:00:21,404 --> 00:00:23,519
and neither aircraft was
supposed to be there.

6
00:00:25,151 --> 00:00:27,608
This is how a bomb,
a bank of low cloud,

7
00:00:27,799 --> 00:00:31,299
and one half-heard radio call put
them on the same strip of concrete.

8
00:00:36,891 --> 00:00:41,229
Los Rodeos was a small regional
airport in the north of Tenerife,

9
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:43,313
in Spain's Canary Islands.

10
00:00:43,764 --> 00:00:46,239
It had one runway and a
modest parking apron.

11
00:00:47,770 --> 00:00:54,151
That morning a KLM Boeing 747,
flight 4805, left Amsterdam,

12
00:00:54,502 --> 00:00:58,979
bound for Las Palmas, on the
neighboring island of Gran Canaria.

13
00:01:00,591 --> 00:01:05,993
A Pan Am 747, flight 1736, had flown
from Los Angeles to New York,

14
00:01:06,224 --> 00:01:08,339
and then set out for
the same destination.

15
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:11,860
During the flight we received word

16
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:13,880
that a bomb explosion
had taken place

17
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:15,940
in the departure hall of the aircraft

18
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:19,150
and consequently the
airport was closed down.

19
00:01:19,630 --> 00:01:21,635
Because of the threat
of a second explosion,

20
00:01:21,655 --> 00:01:24,731
the terminal was evacuated
and the airport was closed.

21
00:01:25,422 --> 00:01:28,919
Many arriving flights were
sent to Los Rodeos instead.

22
00:01:30,391 --> 00:01:32,328
Its parking area filled with airliners.

23
00:01:32,901 --> 00:01:34,859
KLM and Pan Am were among them.

24
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:39,847
When Las Palmas reopened, the
Pan Am crew was ready to go.

25
00:01:40,418 --> 00:01:41,791
But the KLM jet,

26
00:01:41,941 --> 00:01:45,197
which had let its passengers leave
the aircraft during the delay,

27
00:01:45,388 --> 00:01:46,500
was blocking the way.

28
00:01:47,830 --> 00:01:50,785
The KLM crew used the wait to refuel,

29
00:01:50,976 --> 00:01:53,691
taking on fifty-five thousand
five hundred liters.

30
00:01:54,382 --> 00:01:56,406
Investigators believed
the captain wanted

31
00:01:56,426 --> 00:01:58,659
to avoid a second
delay at Las Palmas.

32
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:04,649
The main apron was jammed, so the
jets could not use the parallel taxiway.

33
00:02:05,061 --> 00:02:09,259
To reach the end of the runway, they
had to taxi along the runway itself.

34
00:02:14,971 --> 00:02:18,933
The controllers told the KLM to
taxi to the far end of the runway,

35
00:02:19,284 --> 00:02:20,699
turn around, and wait.

36
00:02:22,271 --> 00:02:26,092
Behind it, the Pan Am was told to
follow along the same runway,

37
00:02:26,423 --> 00:02:28,499
and to leave it by the
third exit on the left.

38
00:02:30,171 --> 00:02:34,271
On the Pan Am cockpit recording, the
crew can be heard counting the exits.

39
00:02:34,882 --> 00:02:37,679
One of them says he thought
the controller had said first.

40
00:02:39,251 --> 00:02:41,746
Meanwhile, low cloud was rolling in.

41
00:02:42,137 --> 00:02:45,225
Runway visibility fell from
two or three kilometers

42
00:02:45,245 --> 00:02:47,239
to three hundred meters
in twelve minutes.

43
00:02:48,872 --> 00:02:51,798
At times the tower could
not see either aircraft.

44
00:02:53,391 --> 00:02:56,025
At the end of the runway,
the KLM first officer said,

45
00:02:56,677 --> 00:02:59,391
"Wait a minute, we don't
have an A T C clearance."

46
00:02:59,822 --> 00:03:02,156
The captain answered,
"No, I know that.

47
00:03:02,407 --> 00:03:03,539
Go ahead, ask."

48
00:03:05,230 --> 00:03:09,027
The tower then read out the route the
aircraft would fly after departure.

49
00:03:09,478 --> 00:03:11,291
That is not permission to take off.

50
00:03:11,782 --> 00:03:15,839
The KLM crew read it back, and
added: "We're now at take off."

51
00:03:17,491 --> 00:03:19,065
The brakes were already off.

52
00:03:19,496 --> 00:03:21,110
The controller said, "Okay."

53
00:03:21,582 --> 00:03:23,527
Then: "Stand by for take off.

54
00:03:23,547 --> 00:03:24,359
I will call you."

55
00:03:26,031 --> 00:03:28,505
At that same moment,
the Pan Am crew radioed:

56
00:03:28,856 --> 00:03:30,850
"We are still taxiing down the runway."

57
00:03:31,581 --> 00:03:33,314
The two transmissions overlapped,

58
00:03:33,425 --> 00:03:35,899
and a shrill whistle
filled the KLM cockpit.

59
00:03:37,311 --> 00:03:40,569
The tower then asked Pan Am to
report when the runway was clear.

60
00:03:41,001 --> 00:03:43,959
Pan Am answered, "Okay,
we'll report when we're clear."

61
00:03:45,590 --> 00:03:49,558
In the KLM cockpit, the flight engineer
asked, "Is he not clear then?"

62
00:03:49,578 --> 00:03:53,033
And again: "Is he not clear,
that Pan American?"

63
00:03:53,544 --> 00:03:56,259
The captain answered,
emphatically, "Oh, yes."

64
00:03:57,830 --> 00:04:01,480
The report says the captain's great
prestige may have kept the others

65
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:02,713
from objecting again.

66
00:04:03,024 --> 00:04:05,440
The impact came about
thirteen seconds later.

67
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:11,080
As far as our information
goes, and I must stress this,

68
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:14,410
that the information has
been rather fragmentary.

69
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:18,460
Our aircraft departed and just about

70
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:23,690
on rotation or just after
collided with the Penn M 747.

71
00:04:24,620 --> 00:04:27,950
And the result of that, as you
can well guess, is disastrous.

72
00:04:32,451 --> 00:04:35,680
The Pan Am crew saw the
KLM jet coming at them out

73
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:38,538
of the fog about nine and a
half seconds before impact.

74
00:04:40,451 --> 00:04:42,326
The captain pushed
the throttles forward,

75
00:04:42,436 --> 00:04:44,932
and his first officer called out, "Get off!

76
00:04:45,143 --> 00:04:45,674
Get off!"

77
00:04:46,426 --> 00:04:47,639
It was not enough time.

78
00:04:49,311 --> 00:04:53,149
The two aircraft collided at about six
minutes past five in the afternoon.

79
00:04:53,581 --> 00:04:57,279
Within minutes, the fog had thinned
to about a kilometer of visibility.

80
00:04:58,751 --> 00:05:03,239
All two hundred and forty-eight
people aboard the KLM aircraft died.

81
00:05:04,651 --> 00:05:05,906
On the Pan Am aircraft,

82
00:05:06,077 --> 00:05:09,198
three hundred and thirty-five
died, and sixty-one survived.

83
00:05:10,832 --> 00:05:13,658
American television was still
counting that Monday evening.

84
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:17,290
It was the worst disaster
in aviation history.

85
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,950
There are at least 578 dead,

86
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:23,900
all of the 248 aboard the
Dutch KLM plane taking off,

87
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:28,410
and at least 330 of the 396
aboard a Pan -American plane.

88
00:05:28,891 --> 00:05:31,286
Fire crews and rescuers
went to the wreckage.

89
00:05:31,817 --> 00:05:34,825
The pictures here are newsreels
and television reports

90
00:05:34,845 --> 00:05:36,899
from the hours and
days that followed.

91
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:42,790
I sat on the part of the wing,
almost falling backwards,

92
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:48,410
but anyway I caught myself and so
I had to jump landing on my feet,

93
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:49,130
which I did.

94
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:51,430
I'm afraid I landed on
somebody's back.

95
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:54,750
I hope and pray that they were safe.

96
00:05:55,311 --> 00:05:58,267
No film of the collision
itself is known to exist.

97
00:05:58,899 --> 00:06:01,705
Where the pictures here are from
another place or another year,

98
00:06:01,725 --> 00:06:03,759
the label on the screen says so.

99
00:06:05,110 --> 00:06:08,807
Pan Am's first officer, Robert
Bragg, was among the survivors.

100
00:06:09,399 --> 00:06:12,635
The United States later gave him
its President's Award for heroism,

101
00:06:12,745 --> 00:06:14,979
for helping fellow crew
members and passengers.

102
00:06:20,631 --> 00:06:24,051
Spain's accident board named
the KLM captain's actions

103
00:06:24,302 --> 00:06:26,439
as the fundamental
cause of the disaster.

104
00:06:28,070 --> 00:06:29,282
It listed four faults.

105
00:06:29,693 --> 00:06:31,125
He took off without clearance.

106
00:06:31,696 --> 00:06:33,539
He did not obey the order to stand by.

107
00:06:33,559 --> 00:06:37,004
He did not stop when Pan Am
reported it was still on the runway.

108
00:06:37,024 --> 00:06:40,619
And he answered the flight
engineer's question with a firm yes.

109
00:06:42,250 --> 00:06:44,966
The board also listed factors
that may have contributed:

110
00:06:45,136 --> 00:06:46,188
the worsening weather,

111
00:06:46,399 --> 00:06:48,693
strict new Dutch limits
on crew duty time,

112
00:06:48,904 --> 00:06:51,719
and the two radio transmissions
made at the same time.

113
00:06:53,331 --> 00:06:55,846
The Dutch authorities disagreed
with parts of the finding.

114
00:06:56,337 --> 00:06:58,632
They said the captain had
taken off prematurely,

115
00:06:58,842 --> 00:07:02,039
but called it a misunderstanding
between the aircraft and the tower.

116
00:07:03,651 --> 00:07:07,578
In fact, the argument over blame
had begun on the first day.

117
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:13,470
KLM experts from Holland said
air traffic controllers had goofed,

118
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:15,540
with the result that
the two planes were

119
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:17,710
on the same runway at the same time.

120
00:07:18,211 --> 00:07:20,245
The Spanish report called for exact,

121
00:07:20,355 --> 00:07:22,691
standard, unequivocal
radio language,

122
00:07:22,861 --> 00:07:26,439
and for avoiding the words "take
off" in an air traffic clearance.

123
00:07:27,971 --> 00:07:29,194
Today, pilots

124
00:07:29,214 --> 00:07:33,588
and controllers say "take off" only
when an aircraft is actually cleared

125
00:07:33,608 --> 00:07:34,099
to go.

126
00:07:35,691 --> 00:07:37,295
Cockpit training changed as well.

127
00:07:37,315 --> 00:07:39,641
Airlines began teaching crews

128
00:07:39,661 --> 00:07:41,575
to speak up when
something seems wrong,

129
00:07:41,806 --> 00:07:43,279
even to a respected captain.

130
00:07:44,591 --> 00:07:47,899
KLM later accepted
responsibility for the accident.

131
00:07:49,311 --> 00:07:51,906
In 2007, on the thirtieth anniversary,

132
00:07:52,257 --> 00:07:55,333
the first official international
memorial service was held,

133
00:07:55,664 --> 00:07:58,419
with relatives from the
Netherlands and the United States.

134
00:08:00,131 --> 00:08:02,848
On a mountainside above
the city stands a monument:

135
00:08:03,279 --> 00:08:06,979
a spiral staircase to the sky,
whose steps stop suddenly.

136
00:08:08,471 --> 00:08:12,899
Fog, a bomb, and a half-heard
call killed 583 people.

137
00:08:14,512 --> 00:08:16,777
One captain's mistake, or everyone's?
